{"timestamp":"2026-09-23T08:19:14.944Z","ordinal":17489,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0cd58-f77f-7d52-8d83-d08a0dc12cae","call_id":"call_cniwv03NGkR62L7PmMmoiqWf","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"PostgreSQL: Documentation: 17: CREATE SERVER (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-createserver.html)\nciteturn99view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-createserver.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 98\nL0: August 13, 2026: cite0†PostgreSQL 18.6, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, 14.24 and 19 Beta 3 Released! L1: \nL2: cite1†Documentation → cite2†PostgreSQL 17 L3: \nL4: Supported Versions: cite3†Current (cite4†18 ) / cite5†17 / cite6†16 / cite7†15 / cite8†14 L5: \nL6: Development Versions: cite9†19 / cite10†devel L7: \nL8: Unsupported versions: cite11†13 / cite12†12 / cite13†11 / cite14†10 / cite15†9.6 / cite16†9.5 / cite17†9.4 / cite18†9.3 / cite19†9.2 / cite20†9.1 / cite21†9.0 / cite22†8.4 L9: \nL10: [Input]\nL11: \nL12: [Input: Search the documentation for...]\nL13: CREATE SERVER\nL14: ---\nL15: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up | SQL Commands  | cite2†Home |  cite25†Next L16: \nL17: * * *\nL18: ## CREATE SERVER\nL19: \nL20: CREATE SERVER — define a new foreign server\nL21: \nL22: ## Synopsis\nL23: \nL24:     CREATE SERVER [ IF NOT EXISTS ] `server_name` [ TYPE '`server_type`' ] [ VERSION '`server_version`' ]\nL25:         FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER `fdw_name`\nL26:         [ OPTIONS ( `option` '`value`' [, ... ] ) ]\nL27: ## Description\nL28: \nL29: `CREATE SERVER` defines a new foreign server. The user who defines the server becomes its owner.\nL30: \nL31: A foreign server typically encapsulates connection information that a foreign-data wrapper uses to access an external data resource. Additional user-specific connection information may be specified by means of user mappings.\nL32: \nL33: The server name must be unique within the database.\nL34: \nL35: Creating a server requires `USAGE` privilege on the foreign-data wrapper being used.\nL36: ## Parameters\nL37: \nL38: `IF NOT EXISTS`\nL39: \nL40: \nL41: Do not throw an error if a server with the same name already exists. A notice is issued in this case. Note that there is no guarantee that the existing server is anything like the one that would have been created.\nL42: \nL43: `server_name`\nL44: \nL45: \nL46: The name of the foreign server to be created.\nL47: \nL48: `server_type`\nL49: \nL50: \nL51: Optional server type, potentially useful to foreign-data wrappers.\nL52: \nL53: `server_version`\nL54: \nL55: \nL56: Optional server version, potentially useful to foreign-data wrappers.\nL57: `fdw_name`\nL58: \nL59: \nL60: The name of the foreign-data wrapper that manages the server.\nL61: \nL62: `OPTIONS ( `option` '`value`' [, ... ] )`\nL63: \nL64: \nL65: This clause specifies the options for the server. The options typically define the connection details of the server, but the actual names and values are dependent on the server's foreign-data wrapper.\nL66: ## Notes\nL67: \nL68: When using the cite26†dblink module, a foreign server's name can be used as an argument of the cite27†dblink_connect function to indicate the connection parameters. It is necessary to have the `USAGE` privilege on the foreign server to be able to use it in this way.\nL69: \nL70: If the foreign server supports sort pushdown, it is necessary for it to have the same sort ordering as the local server.\nL71: ## Examples\nL72: \nL73: Create a server `myserver` that uses the foreign-data wrapper `postgres_fdw`:\nL74: \nL75:     CREATE SERVER myserver FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER postgres_fdw OPTIONS (host 'foo', dbname 'foodb', port '5432');\nL76: \nL77: \nL78: See cite28†postgres_fdw for more details.\nL79: \nL80: ## Compatibility\nL81: \nL82: `CREATE SERVER` conforms to ISO/IEC 9075-9 (SQL/MED).\nL83: ## See Also\nL84: \nL85: cite29†ALTER SERVER , cite30†DROP SERVER , cite31†CREATE FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER , cite32†CREATE FOREIGN TABLE , cite33†CREATE USER MAPPING L86: \nL87: * * *\nL88: \nL89: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up |  cite25†Next L90: CREATE SEQUENCE   | cite2†Home |  CREATE STATISTICS\nL91: ## Submit correction\nL92: \nL93: If you see anything in the documentation that is not correct, does not match your experience with the particular feature or requires further clarification, please use cite34†this form to report a documentation issue.\nL94: \nL95: cite35†Policies | cite36†Code of Conduct | cite37†About PostgreSQL | cite38†Contact L96: \nL97: Copyright © 1996-2026 The PostgreSQL Global Development Group\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPostgreSQL: Documentation: 17: ALTER SERVER (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-alterserver.html)\nciteturn99view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-alterserver.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 87\nL0: August 13, 2026: cite0†PostgreSQL 18.6, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, 14.24 and 19 Beta 3 Released! L1: \nL2: cite1†Documentation → cite2†PostgreSQL 17 L3: \nL4: Supported Versions: cite3†Current (cite4†18 ) / cite5†17 / cite6†16 / cite7†15 / cite8†14 L5: \nL6: Development Versions: cite9†19 / cite10†devel L7: \nL8: Unsupported versions: cite11†13 / cite12†12 / cite13†11 / cite14†10 / cite15†9.6 / cite16†9.5 / cite17†9.4 / cite18†9.3 / cite19†9.2 / cite20†9.1 / cite21†9.0 / cite22†8.4 L9: \nL10: [Input]\nL11: \nL12: [Input: Search the documentation for...]\nL13: ALTER SERVER\nL14: ---\nL15: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up | SQL Commands  | cite2†Home |  cite25†Next L16: \nL17: * * *\nL18: ## ALTER SERVER\nL19: \nL20: ALTER SERVER — change the definition of a foreign server\nL21: \nL22: ## Synopsis\nL23: \nL24:     ALTER SERVER `name` [ VERSION '`new_version`' ]\nL25:         [ OPTIONS ( [ ADD | SET | DROP ] `option` ['`value`'] [, ... ] ) ]\nL26:     ALTER SERVER `name` OWNER TO { `new_owner` | CURRENT_ROLE | CURRENT_USER | SESSION_USER }\nL27:     ALTER SERVER `name` RENAME TO `new_name`\nL28: ## Description\nL29: \nL30: `ALTER SERVER` changes the definition of a foreign server. The first form changes the server version string or the generic options of the server (at least one clause is required). The second form changes the owner of the server.\nL31: To alter the server you must be the owner of the server. Additionally to alter the owner, you must be able to `SET ROLE` to the new owning role, and you must have `USAGE` privilege on the server's foreign-data wrapper. (Note that superusers satisfy all these criteria automatically.)\nL32: ## Parameters\nL33: \nL34: `name`\nL35: \nL36: \nL37: The name of an existing server.\nL38: \nL39: `new_version`\nL40: \nL41: \nL42: New server version.\nL43: \nL44: `OPTIONS ( [ ADD | SET | DROP ] `option` ['`value`'] [, ... ] )`\nL45: \nL46: \nL47: Change options for the server. `ADD`, `SET`, and `DROP` specify the action to be performed. `ADD` is assumed if no operation is explicitly specified. Option names must be unique; names and values are also validated using the server's foreign-data wrapper library.\nL48: \nL49: `new_owner`\nL50: \nL51: \nL52: The user name of the new owner of the foreign server.\nL53: `new_name`\nL54: \nL55: \nL56: The new name for the foreign server.\nL57: ## Examples\nL58: \nL59: Alter server `foo`, add connection options:\nL60: \nL61:     ALTER SERVER foo OPTIONS (host 'foo', dbname 'foodb');\nL62: \nL63: \nL64: Alter server `foo`, change version, change `host` option:\nL65: \nL66:     ALTER SERVER foo VERSION '8.4' OPTIONS (SET host 'baz');\nL67: \nL68: \nL69: ## Compatibility\nL70: \nL71: `ALTER SERVER` conforms to ISO/IEC 9075-9 (SQL/MED). The `OWNER TO` and `RENAME` forms are PostgreSQL extensions.\nL72: ## See Also\nL73: \nL74: cite26†CREATE SERVER , cite27†DROP SERVER L75: \nL76: * * *\nL77: \nL78: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up |  cite25†Next L79: ALTER SEQUENCE   | cite2†Home |  ALTER STATISTICS\nL80: ## Submit correction\nL81: \nL82: If you see anything in the documentation that is not correct, does not match your experience with the particular feature or requires further clarification, please use cite28†this form to report a documentation issue.\nL83: \nL84: cite29†Policies | cite30†Code of Conduct | cite31†About PostgreSQL | cite32†Contact L85: \nL86: Copyright © 1996-2026 The PostgreSQL Global Development Group\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPostgreSQL: Documentation: 17: DROP SERVER (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-dropserver.html)\nciteturn99view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-dropserver.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 77\nL0: August 13, 2026: cite0†PostgreSQL 18.6, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, 14.24 and 19 Beta 3 Released! L1: \nL2: cite1†Documentation → cite2†PostgreSQL 17 L3: \nL4: Supported Versions: cite3†Current (cite4†18 ) / cite5†17 / cite6†16 / cite7†15 / cite8†14 L5: \nL6: Development Versions: cite9†19 / cite10†devel L7: \nL8: Unsupported versions: cite11†13 / cite12†12 / cite13†11 / cite14†10 / cite15†9.6 / cite16†9.5 / cite17†9.4 / cite18†9.3 / cite19†9.2 / cite20†9.1 / cite21†9.0 / cite22†8.4 L9: \nL10: [Input]\nL11: \nL12: [Input: Search the documentation for...]\nL13: DROP SERVER\nL14: ---\nL15: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up | SQL Commands  | cite2†Home |  cite25†Next L16: \nL17: * * *\nL18: ## DROP SERVER\nL19: \nL20: DROP SERVER — remove a foreign server descriptor\nL21: \nL22: ## Synopsis\nL23: \nL24:     DROP SERVER [ IF EXISTS ] `name` [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]\nL25: \nL26: \nL27: ## Description\nL28: \nL29: `DROP SERVER` removes an existing foreign server descriptor. To execute this command, the current user must be the owner of the server.\nL30: ## Parameters\nL31: \nL32: `IF EXISTS`\nL33: \nL34: \nL35: Do not throw an error if the server does not exist. A notice is issued in this case.\nL36: \nL37: `name`\nL38: \nL39: \nL40: The name of an existing server.\nL41: \nL42: `CASCADE`\nL43: \nL44: \nL45: Automatically drop objects that depend on the server (such as user mappings), and in turn all objects that depend on those objects (see cite26†Section 5.15 ).\nL46: \nL47: `RESTRICT`\nL48: \nL49: \nL50: Refuse to drop the server if any objects depend on it. This is the default.\nL51: ## Examples\nL52: \nL53: Drop a server `foo` if it exists:\nL54: \nL55:     DROP SERVER IF EXISTS foo;\nL56: \nL57: \nL58: ## Compatibility\nL59: \nL60: `DROP SERVER` conforms to ISO/IEC 9075-9 (SQL/MED). The `IF EXISTS` clause is a PostgreSQL extension.\nL61: \nL62: ## See Also\nL63: \nL64: cite27†CREATE SERVER , cite28†ALTER SERVER L65: \nL66: * * *\nL67: \nL68: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up |  cite25†Next L69: DROP SEQUENCE   | cite2†Home |  DROP STATISTICS\nL70: ## Submit correction\nL71: \nL72: If you see anything in the documentation that is not correct, does not match your experience with the particular feature or requires further clarification, please use cite29†this form to report a documentation issue.\nL73: \nL74: cite30†Policies | cite31†Code of Conduct | cite32†About PostgreSQL | cite33†Contact L75: \nL76: Copyright © 1996-2026 The PostgreSQL Global Development Group\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPostgreSQL: Documentation: 17: DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-dropforeigndatawrapper.html)\nciteturn99view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-dropforeigndatawrapper.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 77\nL0: August 13, 2026: cite0†PostgreSQL 18.6, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, 14.24 and 19 Beta 3 Released! L1: \nL2: cite1†Documentation → cite2†PostgreSQL 17 L3: \nL4: Supported Versions: cite3†Current (cite4†18 ) / cite5†17 / cite6†16 / cite7†15 / cite8†14 L5: \nL6: Development Versions: cite9†19 / cite10†devel L7: \nL8: Unsupported versions: cite11†13 / cite12†12 / cite13†11 / cite14†10 / cite15†9.6 / cite16†9.5 / cite17†9.4 / cite18†9.3 / cite19†9.2 / cite20†9.1 / cite21†9.0 / cite22†8.4 L9: \nL10: [Input]\nL11: \nL12: [Input: Search the documentation for...]\nL13: DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER\nL14: ---\nL15: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up | SQL Commands  | cite2†Home |  cite25†Next L16: \nL17: * * *\nL18: ## DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER\nL19: \nL20: DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER — remove a foreign-data wrapper\nL21: \nL22: ## Synopsis\nL23: \nL24:     DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER [ IF EXISTS ] `name` [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]\nL25: \nL26: \nL27: ## Description\nL28: \nL29: `DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER` removes an existing foreign-data wrapper. To execute this command, the current user must be the owner of the foreign-data wrapper.\nL30: ## Parameters\nL31: \nL32: `IF EXISTS`\nL33: \nL34: \nL35: Do not throw an error if the foreign-data wrapper does not exist. A notice is issued in this case.\nL36: \nL37: `name`\nL38: \nL39: \nL40: The name of an existing foreign-data wrapper.\nL41: \nL42: `CASCADE`\nL43: \nL44: \nL45: Automatically drop objects that depend on the foreign-data wrapper (such as foreign tables and servers), and in turn all objects that depend on those objects (see cite26†Section 5.15 ).\nL46: \nL47: `RESTRICT`\nL48: \nL49: \nL50: Refuse to drop the foreign-data wrapper if any objects depend on it. This is the default.\nL51: ## Examples\nL52: \nL53: Drop the foreign-data wrapper `dbi`:\nL54: \nL55:     DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER dbi;\nL56: \nL57: \nL58: ## Compatibility\nL59: \nL60: `DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER` conforms to ISO/IEC 9075-9 (SQL/MED). The `IF EXISTS` clause is a PostgreSQL extension.\nL61: \nL62: ## See Also\nL63: \nL64: cite27†CREATE FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER , cite28†ALTER FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER L65: \nL66: * * *\nL67: \nL68: cite23†Prev | cite24†Up |  cite25†Next L69: DROP EXTENSION   | cite2†Home |  DROP FOREIGN TABLE\nL70: ## Submit correction\nL71: \nL72: If you see anything in the documentation that is not correct, does not match your experience with the particular feature or requires further clarification, please use cite29†this form to report a documentation issue.\nL73: \nL74: cite30†Policies | cite31†Code of Conduct | cite32†About PostgreSQL | cite33†Contact L75: \nL76: Copyright © 1996-2026 The PostgreSQL Global Development Group\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"14f088\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005667,\"exit_code\":1,\"original_token_count\":2673,\"output\":\"cat: src/Binding/Statement/Definition/DropRoutines.php: No such file or directory\\n<?php\\n\\ndeclare(strict_types=1);\\n\\nnamespace SqlSemantics\\\\Binding\\\\Statement\\\\Session;\\n\\nuse SqlParser\\\\Parser\\\\Node;\\nuse SqlSemantics\\\\Binding\\\\Query\\\\QueryContext;\\nuse SqlSemantics\\\\Binding\\\\Scope;\\nuse SqlSemantics\\\\Binding\\\\Statement;\\nuse SqlSemantics\\\\Model\\\\BoundStatement;\\nuse SqlSemantics\\\\Model\\\\Statement\\\\Origin;\\n\\n/**\\n * Routes session operations, execution plans and named schema commands.\\n * @visibility SqlSemantics\\n */\\nfinal class ExecutionBinder\\n{\\n    /**\\n     * Returns the first operation whose grammar belongs to an explicit command family.\\n     */\\n    public static function bind(Origin $origin, Node $statement, QueryContext $context): ?BoundStatement\\n    {\\n        $scope = new Scope($context->tables->identifiers, queries: $context);\\n        return Statement\\\\Definition\\\\UserMappings::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Definition\\\\WrapperDeclarations::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Definition\\\\ForeignImports::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Definition\\\\SpatialDefinitions::bind($origin, $statement)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Definition\\\\MySqlRemovals::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Routine\\\\DropRoutines::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Maintenance\\\\IndexCaches::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Maintenance\\\\MySqlTables::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Maintenance\\\\TruncateBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? TableLockBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Inspection\\\\ServerInspection::bind($origin, $statement)\\n            ?? DoBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $scope)\\n            ?? SessionBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $scope)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Prepared\\\\PreparedBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Cursor\\\\CursorBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Plan\\\\ExplainBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $context)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\Transaction\\\\XaBinder::bind($origin, $statement)\\n            ?? (new Statement\\\\TransactionBinder())->bind($origin, $statement, $scope)\\n            ?? (new Statement\\\\MaintenanceBinder())->bind($origin, $statement, $scope)\\n            ?? Statement\\\\ObjectBinder::bind($origin, $statement, $context);\\n    }\\n}\\n<?php\\n\\ndeclare(strict_types=1);\\n\\nnamespace SqlSemantics\\\\Model\\\\Validation;\\n\\n/**\\n * A classified SQL request whose operands cannot form a valid semantic operation.\\n * @visibility public\\n * @example Inspecting an invalid assignment\\n *     \\\\SqlSemantics\\\\Model\\\\Validation\\\\InputViolation::AssignmentWidth->value // => 'assignment-column-count'\\n */\\nenum InputViolation: string\\n{\\n    case MappingRole = 'mapping-role';\\n    case MappingOption = 'mapping-option';\\n    case WrapperFunction = 'wrapper-function';\\n    case WrapperOption = 'wrapper-option';\\n    case RelationName = 'relation-name';\\n    case SpatialReferenceId = 'spatial-reference-id';\\n    case SpatialAttribute = 'spatial-attribute';\\n    case TypeModifier = 'type-modifier';\\n    case RoutineName = 'routine-name';\\n    case AggregateArgumentMode = 'aggregate-argument-mode';\\n    case AggregateVariadicSignature = 'aggregate-variadic-signature';\\n    case DiscardedValue = 'discarded-value';\\n    case HistogramTarget = 'histogram-target';\\n    case HistogramImport = 'histogram-import';\\n    case HistogramBuckets = 'histogram-buckets';\\n    case TemporalOperand = 'temporal-operand';\\n    case TableColumns = 'table-columns';\\n    case XaIdentifier = 'xa-identifier';\\n    case DeleteTarget = 'delete-target';\\n    case ExtractionField = 'extraction-field';\\n    case QueryOperand = 'query-operand';\\n    case FunctionArity = 'function-arity';\\n    case ReindexOption = 'reindex-option';\\n    case ConcurrentSystemReindex = 'concurrent-system-reindex';\\n    case OrderedSetWindow = 'ordered-set-window';\\n    case CteColumnCount = 'cte-column-count';\\n    case DuplicateCte = 'duplicate-cte';\\n    case DefaultContext = 'default-context';\\n    case ForeignKeyWidth = 'foreign-key-column-count';\\n    case ConcurrentIndexDrop = 'invalid-concurrent-index-drop';\\n    case JsonOption = 'json-option';\\n    case XmlOption = 'xml-option';\\n    case CursorOptions = 'cursor-options';\\n    case ScalarQueryWidth = 'scalar-query-width';\\n    case ComparisonWidth = 'comparison-width';\\n    case JoinedMutationPagination = 'joined-mutation-pagination';\\n    case TriggerQualifiedTarget = 'qualified-trigger-target';\\n    case TriggerIndexHint = 'trigger-index-hint';\\n    case AddedColumnKey = 'added-column-key';\\n    case MultiplePrimaryKeys = 'multiple-primary-keys';\\n    case OutputPosition = 'invalid-output-position';\\n    case ExplainOption = 'invalid-explain-option';\\n    case ExplainSetting = 'invalid-explain-setting';\\n    case ExplainCombination = 'invalid-explain-combination';\\n    case AssignmentWidth = 'assignment-column-count';\\n    case TupleSource = 'tuple-source';\\n    case InsertWidth = 'insert-column-count';\\n    case ValuesWidth = 'values-column-count';\\n    case SetWidth = 'set-column-count';\\n    case AlterAlgorithm = 'invalid-alter-algorithm';\\n    case AlterLock = 'invalid-alter-lock';\\n    /** @var array<string, string> */\\n    private const MESSAGES = [\\n        'mapping-role' => 'A user mapping requires a nonempty role name other than the reserved name none.',\\n        'mapping-option' => 'Initial user mapping options require unique names.',\\n        'wrapper-function' => 'A foreign-data wrapper cannot specify a support function more than once.',\\n        'wrapper-option' => 'Initial foreign-data wrapper options require unique names.',\\n        'relation-name' => 'A relation name requires at most three identifier components without subscripts or wildcards.',\\n        'spatial-reference-id' => 'Spatial reference system DDL requires a nonzero unsigned 32-bit SRID and unsigned 32-bit organization identifiers.',\\n        'spatial-attribute' => 'A spatial definition requires exactly one NAME and DEFINITION and no duplicate attributes.',\\n        'type-modifier' => 'PostgreSQL type modifiers require simple numeric or text constants or unqualified identifiers.',\\n        'routine-name' => 'A routine name requires identifier components without subscripts or wildcards.',\\n        'aggregate-argument-mode' => 'An aggregate signature accepts only input and variadic arguments.',\\n        'aggregate-variadic-signature' => 'A variadic direct argument requires one variadic aggregated argument of the same declared type.',\\n        'discarded-value' => 'DO requires scalar expressions and cannot expand table columns without a table input.',\\n        'histogram-target' => 'A histogram request requires exactly one target table.',\\n        'histogram-import' => 'Imported histogram data describes exactly one column.',\\n        'histogram-buckets' => 'A histogram bucket limit must be an integer from 1 to 1024.',\\n        'temporal-operand' => 'Temporal arithmetic requires one scalar temporal input and one scalar interval quantity.',\\n        'table-columns' => 'A table declaration requires at least one column in this SQL dialect.',\\n        'xa-identifier' => 'An XA identifier requires bounded byte-string components and a nonnegative integer format identifier.',\\n        'delete-target' => 'A MySQL DELETE destination requires a named table reference.',\\n        'extraction-field' => 'EXTRACT requires a field defined by the database language.',\\n        'query-operand' => 'A query operand requires a SELECT, VALUES, or TABLE operation.',\\n        'function-arity' => 'The function or conditional operation requires its declared number of arguments.',\\n        'reindex-option' => 'REINDEX requires a known option and a value in its declared domain.',\\n        'concurrent-system-reindex' => 'System-table indexes cannot be rebuilt concurrently.',\\n        'ordered-set-window' => 'An ordered-set aggregate cannot be used as a window function.',\\n        'default-context' => 'DEFAULT requires a column write or a setting assignment.',\\n        'foreign-key-column-count' => 'A foreign key requires matching local and referenced column counts.',\\n        'invalid-concurrent-index-drop' => 'Concurrent index deletion requires one index and cannot cascade.',\\n        'json-option' => 'The JSON option is outside the domain accepted by this operation.',\\n        'xml-option' => 'The XML column option is unknown or specified more than once.',\\n        'cursor-options' => 'A cursor cannot request both SCROLL and NO SCROLL.',\\n        'scalar-query-width' => 'A scalar subquery requires exactly one result column.',\\n        'comparison-width' => 'Compared row operands must have equal widths.',\\n        'joined-mutation-pagination' => 'A MySQL joined mutation cannot specify ORDER BY or LIMIT.',\\n        'qualified-trigger-target' => 'A SQLite trigger mutation requires an unqualified target name.',\\n        'trigger-index-hint' => 'A SQLite trigger mutation cannot specify an index hint.',\\n        'added-column-key' => 'SQLite ADD COLUMN cannot declare a PRIMARY KEY or UNIQUE constraint.',\\n        'multiple-primary-keys' => 'A table can declare only one primary key.',\\n        'invalid-output-position' => 'ORDER BY position is outside the result.',\\n        'invalid-explain-option' => 'The EXPLAIN option is not defined by this database language.',\\n        'invalid-explain-setting' => 'The EXPLAIN option requires a value in its declared domain.',\\n        'invalid-explain-combination' => 'The EXPLAIN options have incompatible execution requirements.',\\n        'assignment-column-count' => 'Assignment destinations and values must have the same width.',\\n        'tuple-source' => 'A tuple assignment requires a row constructor or a row-producing query.',\\n        'insert-column-count' => 'INSERT destinations and input values must have the same width.',\\n        'values-column-count' => 'VALUES rows must have the same width.',\\n        'set-column-count' => 'Set-operation operands must have the same result width.',\\n        'invalid-alter-algorithm' => 'The requested ALTER algorithm is not defined by this database language.',\\n        'invalid-alter-lock' => 'The requested ALTER lock mode is not defined by this database language.',\\n        'cte-column-count' => 'CTE aliases must match the declared query result positions.',\\n        'duplicate-cte' => 'A WITH clause cannot define the same relation name twice.',\\n    ];\\n\\n    /**\\n     * Describes the operand invariant identified by this diagnosis.\\n     */\\n    public function message(): string\\n    {\\n        return self::MESSAGES[$this->value];\\n    }\\n}\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ccb0-75a7-7862-941a-ed4430a5b3b7","create_time":1790151554.943692}}}
