4†HELP Statement L517: \\nL518:       * cite265†USE Statement L519: \\nL520:   * cite266†MySQL Data Dictionary L521: \\nL522:   * cite267†The InnoDB Storage Engine L523: \\nL524:   * cite268†Alternative Storage Engines L525: \\nL526:   * cite269†Replication L527: \\nL528:   * cite270†Group Replication L529: \\nL530:   * cite271†MySQL Shell L531: \\nL532:   * cite272†Using MySQL as a Document Store L533:   * cite273†InnoDB Cluster L534: \\nL535:   * cite274†InnoDB ReplicaSet L536: \\nL537:   * cite275†MySQL NDB Cluster 8.4 L538: \\nL539:   * cite276†Partitioning L540: \\nL541:   * cite277†Stored Objects L542: \\nL543:   * cite278†INFORMATION_SCHEMA Tables L544: \\nL545:   * cite279†MySQL Performance Schema L546: \\nL547:   * cite280†MySQL sys Schema L548: \\nL549:   * cite281†Connectors and APIs L550: \\nL551:   * cite282†MySQL Enterprise Edition L552: \\nL553:   * cite283†MySQL Workbench L554: \\nL555:   * cite284†MySQL on OCI Marketplace L556: \\nL557:   * cite285†Telemetry L558: \\nL559:   * cite286†MySQL 8.4 Frequently Asked Questions L560: \\nL561:   * cite287†Error Messages and Common Problems L562: \\nL563:   * cite288†Indexes L564:   * cite289†MySQL Glossary L565: \\nL566: Related Documentation\\nL567: \\nL568: cite290†MySQL 8.4 Release Notes L569: \\nL570: Download this Manual\\nL571: \\nL572: cite291†PDF (US Ltr)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.2Mb\\nL573: cite292†PDF (A4)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.3Mb\\nL574: cite293†Man Pages (TGZ)†downloads.mysql.com - 261.9Kb\\nL575: cite294†Man Pages (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 367.5Kb\\nL576: cite295†Info (Gzip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\\nL577: cite296†Info (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\\nL578: \\nL579: version 8.4\\nL580: \\nL581: cite297†26.7 L582: cite298†9.7  current L583: cite299†8.0 L584: \\nL585: cite300†8.0  Japanese L586: cite301†MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual /  ...  /  CREATE DATABASE Statement\\nL587: ### 15.1.12 CREATE DATABASE Statement\\nL588: \\nL589:     `CREATE {DATABASE | SCHEMA} [IF NOT EXISTS] db_name\\nL590:         [create_option] ...\\nL591: \\nL592:     create_option: [DEFAULT] {\\nL593:         CHARACTER SET [=] charset_name\\nL594:       | COLLATE [=] collation_name\\nL595:       | ENCRYPTION [=] {'Y' | 'N'}\\nL596:     }`\\nL597: \\nL598: cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` creates a database with the given name. To use this statement, you need the cite302†`CREATE` privilege for the database. cite36†`CREATE SCHEMA` is a synonym for cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` .\\nL599: An error occurs if the database exists and you did not specify `IF NOT EXISTS`.\\nL600: \\nL601: cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` is not permitted within a session that has an active cite116†`LOCK TABLES` statement.\\nL602: \\nL603: Each `create_option` specifies a database characteristic. Database characteristics are stored in the data dictionary.\\nL604:   * The `CHARACTER SET` option specifies the default database character set. The `COLLATE` option specifies the default database collation. For information about character set and collation names, see cite17†Chapter 12, Character Sets, Collations, Unicode .\\nL605: To see the available character sets and collations, use the the cite212†`SHOW CHARACTER SET` and cite213†`SHOW COLLATION` statements, respectively. See cite212†Section 15.7.7.4, “SHOW CHARACTER SET Statement” , and cite213†Section 15.7.7.5, “SHOW COLLATION Statement” .\\nL606:   * The `ENCRYPTION` option defines the default database encryption, which is inherited by tables created in the database. The permitted values are `'Y'` (encryption enabled) and `'N'` (encryption disabled). If the `ENCRYPTION` option is not specified, the value of the cite303†`default_table_encryption` system variable defines the default database encryption.\\nL607: If the cite304†`table_encryption_privilege_check` system variable is enabled, the cite305†`TABLE_ENCRYPTION_ADMIN` privilege is required to specify a default encryption setting that differs from the cite303†`default_table_encryption` setting. For more information, see cite306†Defining an Encryption Default for Schemas and General Tablespaces .\\nL608: A database in MySQL is implemented as a directory containing files that correspond to tables in the database. Because there are no tables in a database when it is initially created, the cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` statement creates only a directory under the MySQL data directory. Rules for permissible database names are given in cite307†Section 11.2, “Schema Object Names” .\\nL609: If a database name contains special characters, the name for the database directory contains encoded versions of those characters as described in cite308†Section 11.2.4, “Mapping of Identifiers to File Names” .\\nL610: Creating a database directory by manually creating a directory under the data directory (for example, with mkdir) is unsupported in MySQL 8.4.\\nL611: \\nL612: When you create a database, let the server manage the directory and the files in it. Manipulating database directories and files directly can cause inconsistencies and unexpected results.\\nL613: \\nL614: MySQL has no limit on the number of databases. The underlying file system may have a limit on the number of directories.\\nL615: You can also use the cite309†mysqladmin program to create databases. See cite309†Section 6.5.2, “mysqladmin — A MySQL Server Administration Program” .\\nL616: \\nL617: cite35†PREV cite310†HOME cite21†UP cite37†NEXT L618: \\nL619: Related Documentation\\nL620: \\nL621: cite290†MySQL 8.4 Release Notes L622: \\nL623: Download this Manual\\nL624: cite291†PDF (US Ltr)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.2Mb\\nL625: cite292†PDF (A4)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.3Mb\\nL626: cite293†Man Pages (TGZ)†downloads.mysql.com - 261.9Kb\\nL627: cite294†Man Pages (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 367.5Kb\\nL628: cite295†Info (Gzip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\\nL629: cite296†Info (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\\nL630: \\nL631: Contact MySQL Sales\\nL632: USA/Canada: +1-866-221-0634   (cite311†More Countries »†www.mysql.com )\\nL633: \\nL634:   cite312†© 2026 Oracle†www.oracle.com L635:   * cite313†Products†www.mysql.com L636:   * cite314†MySQL HeatWave†www.mysql.com L637:   * cite315†MySQL Enterprise Edition†www.mysql.com L638:   * cite316†MySQL Standard Edition†www.mysql.com L639:   * cite317†MySQL Classic Edition†www.mysql.com L640:   * cite318†MySQL Cluster CGE†www.mysql.com L641:   * cite319†MySQL Embedded (OEM/ISV)†www.mysql.com L642: \\nL643:   * cite320†Services†www.mysql.com L644:   * cite321†Training†www.mysql.com L645:   * cite322†Certification†www.mysql.com L646:   * cite323†Support†www.mysql.com L647:   * cite2†Downloads†www.mysql.com L648:   * cite324†MySQL Community Server L649:   * cite325†MySQL NDB Cluster L650:   * cite326†MySQL Shell L651:   * cite327†MySQL Router L652:   * cite328†MySQL Workbench L653: \\nL654:   * cite4†Documentation L655:   * cite329†MySQL Reference Manual L656:   * cite330†MySQL Workbench L657:   * cite331†MySQL NDB Cluster L658:   * cite332†MySQL Connectors L659:   * cite333†Topic Guides L660:   * cite334†About MySQL†www.mysql.com L661:   * cite335†Contact Us†www.mysql.com L662:   * cite336†Blogs†blogs.oracle.com L663:   * cite337†How to Buy†www.mysql.com L664:   * cite338†Partners†www.mysql.com L665:   * cite339†Job Opportunities†www.mysql.com L666:   * cite340†Site Map†www.mysql.com L667: \\nL668:   cite312†© 2026 Oracle†www.oracle.com L669: \\nL670: cite341†Privacy†www.oracle.com / cite342†Do Not Sell My Info†www.oracle.com | cite343†Terms of Use†www.oracle.com | cite344†Trademark Policy†www.oracle.com |\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMySQL :: MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual :: 15.1.2 ALTER DATABAS