\nL315:         * cite159†Cursor FETCH Statement L316: \nL317:         * cite160†Cursor OPEN Statement L318: \nL319:         * cite161†Restrictions on Server-Side Cursors L320: \nL321:       * cite162†Condition Handling L322: \nL323:         * cite163†DECLARE ... CONDITION Statement L324: \nL325:         * cite164†DECLARE ... HANDLER Statement L326: \nL327:         * cite165†GET DIAGNOSTICS Statement L328: \nL329:         * cite166†RESIGNAL Statement L330: \nL331:         * cite167†SIGNAL Statement L332: \nL333:         * cite168†Scope Rules for Handlers L334:         * cite169†The MySQL Diagnostics Area L335: \nL336:         * cite170†Condition Handling and OUT or INOUT Parameters L337: \nL338:       * cite171†Restrictions on Condition Handling L339: \nL340:     * cite172†Database Administration Statements L341: \nL342:       * cite173†Account Management Statements L343: \nL344:         * cite174†ALTER USER Statement L345: \nL346:         * cite175†CREATE ROLE Statement L347: \nL348:         * cite176†CREATE USER Statement L349: \nL350:         * cite177†DROP ROLE Statement L351: \nL352:         * cite178†DROP USER Statement L353: \nL354:         * cite179†GRANT Statement L355:         * cite180†RENAME USER Statement L356: \nL357:         * cite181†REVOKE Statement L358: \nL359:         * cite182†SET DEFAULT ROLE Statement L360: \nL361:         * cite183†SET PASSWORD Statement L362: \nL363:         * cite184†SET ROLE Statement L364: \nL365:       * cite185†Resource Group Management Statements L366: \nL367:         * cite186†ALTER RESOURCE GROUP Statement L368: \nL369:         * cite187†CREATE RESOURCE GROUP Statement L370: \nL371:         * cite188†DROP RESOURCE GROUP Statement L372: \nL373:         * cite189†SET RESOURCE GROUP Statement L374: \nL375:       * cite190†Table Maintenance Statements L376:         * cite191†ANALYZE TABLE Statement L377: \nL378:         * cite192†CHECK TABLE Statement L379: \nL380:         * cite193†CHECKSUM TABLE Statement L381: \nL382:         * cite194†OPTIMIZE TABLE Statement L383: \nL384:         * cite195†REPAIR TABLE Statement …1136 tokens truncated…    * cite263†EXPLAIN Statement L515: \nL516:       * cite264†HELP Statement L517: \nL518:       * cite265†USE Statement L519: \nL520:   * cite266†MySQL Data Dictionary L521: \nL522:   * cite267†The InnoDB Storage Engine L523: \nL524:   * cite268†Alternative Storage Engines L525: \nL526:   * cite269†Replication L527: \nL528:   * cite270†Group Replication L529: \nL530:   * cite271†MySQL Shell L531: \nL532:   * cite272†Using MySQL as a Document Store L533:   * cite273†InnoDB Cluster L534: \nL535:   * cite274†InnoDB ReplicaSet L536: \nL537:   * cite275†MySQL NDB Cluster 8.4 L538: \nL539:   * cite276†Partitioning L540: \nL541:   * cite277†Stored Objects L542: \nL543:   * cite278†INFORMATION_SCHEMA Tables L544: \nL545:   * cite279†MySQL Performance Schema L546: \nL547:   * cite280†MySQL sys Schema L548: \nL549:   * cite281†Connectors and APIs L550: \nL551:   * cite282†MySQL Enterprise Edition L552: \nL553:   * cite283†MySQL Workbench L554: \nL555:   * cite284†MySQL on OCI Marketplace L556: \nL557:   * cite285†Telemetry L558: \nL559:   * cite286†MySQL 8.4 Frequently Asked Questions L560: \nL561:   * cite287†Error Messages and Common Problems L562: \nL563:   * cite288†Indexes L564:   * cite289†MySQL Glossary L565: \nL566: Related Documentation\nL567: \nL568: cite290†MySQL 8.4 Release Notes L569: \nL570: Download this Manual\nL571: \nL572: cite291†PDF (US Ltr)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.2Mb\nL573: cite292†PDF (A4)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.3Mb\nL574: cite293†Man Pages (TGZ)†downloads.mysql.com - 261.9Kb\nL575: cite294†Man Pages (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 367.5Kb\nL576: cite295†Info (Gzip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\nL577: cite296†Info (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\nL578: \nL579: version 8.4\nL580: \nL581: cite297†26.7 L582: cite298†9.7  current L583: cite299†8.0 L584: \nL585: cite300†8.0  Japanese L586: cite301†MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual /  ...  /  ALTER DATABASE Statement\nL587: ### 15.1.2 ALTER DATABASE Statement\nL588: \nL589:     `ALTER {DATABASE | SCHEMA} [db_name]\nL590:         alter_option ...\nL591: \nL592:     alter_option: {\nL593:         [DEFAULT] CHARACTER SET [=] charset_name\nL594:       | [DEFAULT] COLLATE [=] collation_name\nL595:       | [DEFAULT] ENCRYPTION [=] {'Y' | 'N'}\nL596:       | READ ONLY [=] {DEFAULT | 0 | 1}\nL597:     }`\nL598: cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` enables you to change the overall characteristics of a database. These characteristics are stored in the data dictionary. This statement requires the cite302†`ALTER` privilege on the database. cite23†`ALTER SCHEMA` is a synonym for cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` .\nL599: \nL600: If the database name is omitted, the statement applies to the default database. In that case, an error occurs if there is no default database.\nL601: For any `alter_option` omitted from the statement, the database retains its current option value, with the exception that changing the character set may change the collation and vice versa.\nL602: \nL603:   * cite303†Character Set and Collation Options L604: \nL605:   * cite304†Encryption Option L606: \nL607:   * cite305†Read Only Option L608: #### Character Set and Collation Options\nL609: \nL610: The `CHARACTER SET` option changes the default database character set. The `COLLATE` option changes the default database collation. For information about character set and collation names, see cite17†Chapter 12, Character Sets, Collations, Unicode .\nL611: To see the available character sets and collations, use the cite212†`SHOW CHARACTER SET` and cite213†`SHOW COLLATION` statements, respectively. See cite212†Section 15.7.7.4, “SHOW CHARACTER SET Statement” , and cite213†Section 15.7.7.5, “SHOW COLLATION Statement” .\nL612: A stored routine that uses the database defaults when the routine is created includes those defaults as part of its definition. (In a stored routine, variables with character data types use the database defaults if the character set or collation are not specified explicitly. See cite41†Section 15.1.17, “CREATE PROCEDURE and CREATE FUNCTION Statements” .) If you change the default character set or collation for a database, any stored routines that are to use the new defaults must be dropped and recreated.\nL613: #### Encryption Option\nL614: \nL615: The `ENCRYPTION` option, defines the default database encryption, which is inherited by tables created in the database. The permitted values are `'Y'` (encryption enabled) and `'N'` (encryption disabled).\nL616: The `mysql` system schema cannot be set to default encryption. The existing tables within it are part of the general `mysql` tablespace, which may be encrypted. The `information_schema` contains only views. It is not possible to create any tables within it. There is nothing on the disk to encrypt. All tables in the `performance_schema` use the cite279†`PERFORMANCE_SCHEMA` engine, which is purely in-memory. It is not possible to create any other tables in it. There is nothing on the disk to encrypt.\nL617: Only newly created tables inherit the default database encryption. For existing tables associated with the database, their encryption remains unchanged. If the cite306†`table_encryption_privilege_check` system variable is enabled, the cite307†`TABLE_ENCRYPTION_ADMIN` privilege is required to specify a default encryption setting that differs from the value of the cite308†`default_table_encryption` system variable. For more information, see cite309†Defining an Encryption Default for Schemas and General Tablespaces .\nL618: #### Read Only Option\nL619: \nL620: The `READ ONLY` option controls whether to permit modification of the database and objects within it. The permitted values are `DEFAULT` or `0` (not read only) and `1` (read only). This option is useful for database migration because a database for which `READ ONLY` is enabled can be migrated to another MySQL instance without concern that the database might be changed during the operation.\nL621: With NDB Cluster, making a database read only on one cite310†mysqld server is synchronized to other cite310†mysqld servers in the same cluster, so that the database becomes read only on all cite310†mysqld servers.\nL622: \nL623: The `READ ONLY` option, if enabled, is displayed in the `INFORMATION_SCHEMA` cite311†`SCHEMATA_EXTENSIONS` table. See cite311†Section 28.3.32, “The INFORMATION_SCHEMA SCHEMATA_EXTENSIONS Table” .\nL624: The `READ ONLY` option cannot be enabled for these system schemas: `mysql`, `information_schema`, `performance_schema`.\nL625: \nL626: In cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` statements, the `READ ONLY` option interacts with other instances of itself and with other options as follows:\nL627: \nL628:   * An error occurs if multiple instances of `READ ONLY` conflict (for example, `READ ONLY = 1 READ ONLY = 0`).\nL629: \nL630:   * An cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` statement that contains only (nonconflicting) `READ ONLY` options is permitted even for a read-only database.\nL631:   * A mix of (nonconflicting) `READ ONLY` options with other options is permitted if the read-only state of the database either before or after the statement permits modifications. If the read-only state both before and after prohibits changes, an error occurs.\nL632: \nL633: This statement succeeds whether or not the database is read only:\nL634: \nL635:     `ALTER DATABASE mydb READ ONLY = 0 DEFAULT COLLATE utf8mb4_bin;`\nL636: This statement succeeds if the database is not read only, but fails if it is already read only:\nL637: \nL638:     `ALTER DATABASE mydb READ ONLY = 1 DEFAULT COLLATE utf8mb4_bin;`\nL639: \nL640: Enabling `READ ONLY` affects all users of the database, with these exceptions that are not subject to read-only checks:\nL641: \nL642:   * Statements executed by the server as part of server initialization, restart, upgrade, or replication.\nL643: \nL644:   * Statements in a file named at server startup by the cite312†`init_file` system variable.\nL645:   * `TEMPORARY` tables; it is possible to create, alter, drop, and write to `TEMPORARY` tables in a read-only database.\nL646: \nL647:   * NDB Cluster non-SQL inserts and updates.\nL648: \nL649: Other than for the excepted operations just listed, enabling `READ ONLY` prohibits write operations to the database and its objects, including their definitions, data, and metadata. The following list details affected SQL statements and operations:\nL650: \nL651:   * The database itself:\nL652: \nL653:     * cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` L654:     * cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` (except to change the `READ ONLY` option)\nL655: \nL656:     * cite60†`DROP DATABASE` L657: \nL658:   * Views:\nL659: \nL660:     * cite59†`CREATE VIEW` L661: \nL662:     * cite35†`ALTER VIEW` L663: \nL664:     * cite71†`DROP VIEW` L665: \nL666:     * Selecting from views that invoke functions with side effects.\nL667: \nL668:     * Updating updatable views.\nL669: \nL670:     * Statements that create or drop objects in a writable database are rejected if they affect metadata of a view in a read-only database (for example, by making the view valid or invalid).\nL671: \nL672:   * Stored routines:\nL673:     * cite41†`CREATE PROCEDURE` L674: \nL675:     * cite65†`DROP PROCEDURE` L676: \nL677:     * cite75†`CALL` (of procedures with side effects)\nL678: \nL679:     * cite38†`CREATE FUNCTION` L680: \nL681:     * cite62†`DROP FUNCTION` L682: \nL683:     * cite90†`SELECT` (of functions with side effects)\nL684:     * For procedures and functions, read-only checks follow prelocking behavior. For cite75†`CALL` statements, read-only checks are done on a per-statement basis, so if some conditionally executed statement writing to a read-only database does not actually execute, the call still succeeds. On the other hand, for a function called within a cite90†`SELECT` , execution of the function body happens in prelocked mode.\nL685: As long as a some statement within the function writes to a read-only database, execution of the function fails with an error regardless of whether the statement actually executes.\nL686:   * Triggers:\nL687: \nL688:     * cite58†`CREATE TRIGGER` L689: \nL690:     * cite70†`DROP TRIGGER` L691: \nL692:     * Trigger invocation.\nL693: \nL694:   * Events:\nL695: \nL696:     * cite37†`CREATE EVENT` L697: \nL698:     * cite24†`ALTER EVENT` L699: \nL700:     * cite61†`DROP EVENT` L701: \nL702:     * Event execution:\nL703: \nL704:       * Executing an event in the database fails because that would change the last-execution timestamp, which is event metadata stored in the data dictionary. Failure of event execution also has the effect of causing the event scheduler to stop.\nL705:       * If an event writes to an object in a read-only database, execution of the event fails with an error, but the event scheduler is not stopped.\nL706: \nL707:   * Tables:\nL708: \nL709:     * cite44†`CREATE TABLE` L710: \nL711:     * cite30†`ALTER TABLE` L712: \nL713:     * cite39†`CREATE INDEX` L714: \nL715:     * cite63†`DROP INDEX` L716: \nL717:     * cite72†`RENAME TABLE` L718: \nL719:     * cite73†`TRUNCATE TABLE` L720: \nL721:     * cite68†`DROP TABLE` L722: \nL723:     * cite76†`DELETE` L724: \nL725:     * cite81†`INSERT` L726: \nL727:     * cite80†`IMPORT TABLE` L728: \nL729:     * cite86†`LOAD DATA` L730: \nL731:     * cite87†`LOAD XML` L732: \nL733:     * cite89†`REPLACE` L734: \nL735:     * cite106†`UPDATE` L736:     * For cascading foreign keys where the child table is in a read-only database, updates and deletes on the parent are rejected even if the child table is not directly affected.\nL737: \nL738:     * For a `MERGE` table such as `CREATE TABLE s1.t(i int) ENGINE MERGE UNION (s2.t, s3.t), INSERT_METHOD=...`, the following behavior applies:\nL739:       * Inserting into the `MERGE` table (`INSERT into s1.t`) fails if at least one of `s1`, `s2`, `s3` is read only, regardless of insert method. The insert is refused even if it would actually end up in a writable table.\nL740: \nL741:       * Dropping the `MERGE` table (`DROP TABLE s1.t`) succeeds as long as `s1` is not read only. It is permitted to drop a `MERGE` table that refers to a read-only database.\nL742: An cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` statement blocks until all concurrent transactions that have already accessed an object in the database being altered have committed. Conversely, a write transaction accessing an object in a database being altered in a concurrent cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` blocks until the cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` has committed.\nL743: If the Clone plugin is used to clone a local or remote data directory, the databases in the clone retain the read-only state they had in the source data directory. The read-only state does not affect the cloning process itself. If it is not desirable to have the same database read-only state in the clone, the option must be changed explicitly for the clone after the cloning process has finished, using cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` operations on the clone.\nL744: When cloning from a donor to a recipient, if the recipient has a user database that is read only, cloning fails with an error message. Cloning may be retried after making the database writable.\nL745: `READ ONLY` is permitted for cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` , but not for cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` . However, for a read-only database, the statement produced by cite215†`SHOW CREATE DATABASE` does include `READ ONLY=1` within a comment to indicate its read-only status:\nL746: \nL747:     `mysql> ALTER DATABASE mydb READ ONLY = 1;\nL748:     mysql> SHOW CREATE DATABASE mydb\\G\nL749:     *************************** 1. row ***************************\nL750:            Database: mydb\nL751:     Create Database: CREATE DATABASE `mydb`\nL752:                      /*!40100 DEFAULT CHARACTER SET utf8mb4\nL753:                               COLLATE utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci */\nL754:                      /*!80016 DEFAULT ENCRYPTION='N' */\nL755:                      /* READ ONLY = 1 */`\nL756: If the server executes a cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` statement containing such a comment, the server ignores the comment and the `READ ONLY` option is not processed. This has implications for cite313†mysqldump , which uses cite215†`SHOW CREATE DATABASE` to produce cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` statements in dump output:\nL757: \nL758:   * In a dump file, the cite36†`CREATE DATABASE` statement for a read-only database contains the commented `READ ONLY` option.\nL759:   * The dump file can be restored as usual, but because the server ignores the commented `READ ONLY` option, the restored database is not read only. If the database is to be read only after being restored, you must execute cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` manually to make it so.\nL760: \nL761: Suppose that `mydb` is read only and you dump it as follows:\nL762: \nL763:     `$> mysqldump --databases mydb > mydb.sql`\nL764: A restore operation later must be followed by cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` if `mydb` should still be read only:\nL765: \nL766:     `$> mysql\nL767:     mysql> SOURCE mydb.sql;\nL768:     mysql> ALTER DATABASE mydb READ ONLY = 1;`\nL769: \nL770: MySQL Enterprise Backup is not subject to this issue. It backs up and restores a read-only database like any other, but enables the `READ ONLY` option at restore time if it was enabled at backup time.\nL771: cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` is written to the binary log, so a change to the `READ ONLY` option on a replication source server also affects replicas. To prevent this from happening, binary logging must be disabled prior to execution of the cite23†`ALTER DATABASE` statement. For example, to prepare for migrating a database without affecting replicas, perform these operations:\nL772:   1. Within a single session, disable binary logging and enable `READ ONLY` for the database:\nL773: \nL774:     `mysql> SET sql_log_bin = OFF;\nL775:     mysql> ALTER DATABASE mydb READ ONLY = 1;`\nL776: \nL777:   2. Dump the database, for example, with cite313†mysqldump :\nL778: \nL779:     `$> mysqldump --databases mydb > mydb.sql`\nL780: \nL781:   3. Within a single session, disable binary logging and disable `READ ONLY` for the database:\nL782: \nL783:     `mysql> SET sql_log_bin = OFF;\nL784:     mysql> ALTER DATABASE mydb READ ONLY = 0;`\nL785: cite22†PREV cite314†HOME cite21†UP cite24†NEXT L786: \nL787: Related Documentation\nL788: \nL789: cite290†MySQL 8.4 Release Notes L790: \nL791: Download this Manual\nL792: \nL793: cite291†PDF (US Ltr)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.2Mb\nL794: cite292†PDF (A4)†downloads.mysql.com - 40.3Mb\nL795: cite293†Man Pages (TGZ)†downloads.mysql.com - 261.9Kb\nL796: cite294†Man Pages (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 367.5Kb\nL797: cite295†Info (Gzip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\nL798: cite296†Info (Zip)†downloads.mysql.com - 4.0Mb\nL799: \nL800: Contact MySQL Sales\nL801: USA/Canada: +1-866-221-0634   (cite315†More Countries »†www.mysql.com )\nL802:   cite316†© 2026 Oracle†www.oracle.com L803: \nL804:   * cite317†Products†www.mysql.com L805:   * cite318†MySQL HeatWave†www.mysql.com L806:   * cite319†MySQL Enterprise Edition†www.mysql.com L807:   * cite320†MySQL Standard Edition†www.mysql.com L808:   * cite321†MySQL Classic Edition†www.mysql.com L809:   * cite322†MySQL Cluster CGE†www.mysql.com L810:   * cite323†MySQL Embedded (OEM/ISV)†www.mysql.com L811: \nL812:   * cite324†Services†www.mysql.com L813:   * cite325†Training†www.mysql.com L814:   * cite326†Certification†www.mysql.com L815:   * cite327†Support†www.mysql.com L816:   * cite2†Downloads†www.mysql.com L817:   * cite328†MySQL Community Server L818:   * cite329†MySQL NDB Cluster L819:   * cite330†MySQL Shell L820:   * cite331†MySQL Router L821:   * cite332†MySQL Workbench L822: \nL823:   * cite4†Documentation L824:   * cite333†MySQL Reference Manual L825:   * cite334†MySQL Workbench L826:   * cite335†MySQL NDB Cluster L827:   * cite336†MySQL Connectors L828:   * cite337†Topic Guides L829:   * cite338†About MySQL†www.mysql.com L830:   * cite339†Contact Us†www.mysql.com L831:   * cite340†Blogs†blogs.oracle.com L832:   * cite341†How to Buy†www.mysql.com L833:   * cite342†Partners†www.mysql.com L834:   * cite343†Job Opportunities†www.mysql.com L835:   * cite344†Site Map†www.mysql.com L836: \nL837:   cite316†© 2026 Oracle†www.oracle.com L838: \nL839: cite345†Privacy†www.oracle.com / cite346†Do Not Sell My Info†www.oracle.com | cite347†Terms of Use†www.oracle.com | cite348†Trademark Policy†www.oracle.com |\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ccb0-75a7-7862-941a-ed4430a5b3b7","create_time":1790152150.927514}}}
