\n  Errors               0     \\n -------------------- ------ \\n\\nTime: 7.83 secs; Memory: 46MB\\nGenerating optimized autoload files\\nGenerated optimized autoload files containing 6369 classes\\n 1410/1410 [▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓] 100%\\n\\n\\nFound 0 of 1410 files that can be fixed in 0.798 seconds, 48.00 MB memory used\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":\"PostgreSQL: Documentation: 17: ALTER EVENT TRIGGER (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-altereventtrigger.html)\\nciteturn106view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-altereventtrigger.html\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 76\\nL0: August 13, 2026: cite0†PostgreSQL 18.6, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, 14.24 and 19 Beta 3 Released! L1: \\nL2: cite1†Documentation → cite2†PostgreSQL 17 L3: \\nL4: Supported Versions: cite3†Current (cite4†18 ) / cite5†17 / cite6†16 / cite7†15 / cite8†14 L5: \\nL6: Development Versions: cite9†19 / cite10†devel L7: \\nL8: Unsupported versions: cite11†13 / cite12†12 / cite13†11 / cite14†10 / cite15†9.6 / cite16†9.5 / cite17†9.4 / cite18†9.3 L9: \\nL10: [Input]\\nL11: \\nL12: [Input: Search the documentation for...]\\nL13: \\nL14: ALTER EVENT TRIGGER\\nL15: ---\\nL16: cite19†Prev | cite20†Up | SQL Commands  | cite2†Home |  cite21†Next L17: \\nL18: * * *\\nL19: ## ALTER EVENT TRIGGER\\nL20: \\nL21: ALTER EVENT TRIGGER — change the definition of an event trigger\\nL22: \\nL23: ## Synopsis\\nL24: \\nL25:     ALTER EVENT TRIGGER `name` DISABLE\\nL26:     ALTER EVENT TRIGGER `name` ENABLE [ REPLICA | ALWAYS ]\\nL27:     ALTER EVENT TRIGGER `name` OWNER TO { `new_owner` | CURRENT_ROLE | CURRENT_USER | SESSION_USER }\\nL28:     ALTER EVENT TRIGGER `name` RENAME TO `new_name`\\nL29: \\nL30: \\nL31: ## Description\\nL32: \\nL33: `ALTER EVENT TRIGGER` changes properties of an existing event trigger.\\nL34: \\nL35: You must be superuser to alter an event trigger.\\nL36: ## Parameters\\nL37: \\nL38: `name`\\nL39: \\nL40: \\nL41: The name of an existing trigger to alter.\\nL42: \\nL43: `new_owner`\\nL44: \\nL45: \\nL46: The user name of the new owner of the event trigger.\\nL47: \\nL48: `new_name`\\nL49: \\nL50: \\nL51: The new name of the event trigger.\\nL52: \\nL53: `DISABLE`/`ENABLE [ REPLICA | ALWAYS ]`\\nL54: \\nL55: \\nL56: These forms configure the firing of event triggers. A disabled trigger is still known to the system, but is not executed when its triggering event occurs. See also cite22†session_replication_role .\\nL57: ## Compatibility\\nL58: \\nL59: There is no `ALTER EVENT TRIGGER` statement in the SQL standard.\\nL60: \\nL61: ## See Also\\nL62: \\nL63: cite23†CREATE EVENT TRIGGER , cite24†DROP EVENT TRIGGER L64: \\nL65: * * *\\nL66: \\nL67: cite19†Prev | cite20†Up |  cite21†Next L68: ALTER DOMAIN   | cite2†Home |  ALTER EXTENSION\\nL69: ## Submit correction\\nL70: \\nL71: If you see anything in the documentation that is not correct, does not match your experience with the particular feature or requires further clarification, please use cite25†this form to report a documentation issue.\\nL72: \\nL73: cite26†Policies | cite27†Code of Conduct | cite28†About PostgreSQL | cite29†Contact L74: \\nL75: Copyright © 1996-2026 The PostgreSQL Global Development Group\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nPostgreSQL: Documentation: 17: ALTER EXTENSION (https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-alterextension.html)\\nciteturn106view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\\\"ref_id\\\":\\\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/sql-alterextension.html\\\",\\\"lineno\\\":null}); Total lines: 187\\nL0: August 13, 2026: cite0†PostgreSQL 18.6, 17.11, 16.15, 15.19, 14.24 and 19 Beta 3 Released! L1: \\nL2: cite1†Documentation → cite2†PostgreSQL 17 L3: \\nL4: Supported Versions: cite3†Current (cite4†18 ) / cite5†17 / cite6†16 / cite7†15 / cite8†14 L5: \\nL6: Development Versions: cite9†19 / cite10†devel L7: \\nL8: Unsupported versions: cite11†13 / cite12†12 / cite13†11 / cite14†10 / cite15†9.6 / cite16†9.5 / cite17†9.4 / cite18†9.3 / cite19†9.2 / cite20†9.1 L9: \\nL10: [Input]\\nL11: \\nL12: [Input: Search the documentation for...]\\nL13: ALTER EXTENSION\\nL14: ---\\nL15: cite21†Prev | cite22†Up | SQL Commands  | cite2†Home |  cite23†Next L16: \\nL17: * * *\\nL18: ## ALTER EXTENSION\\nL19: \\nL20: ALTER EXTENSION — change the definition of an extension\\nL21: ## Synopsis\\nL22: \\nL23:     ALTER EXTENSION `name` UPDATE [ TO `new_version` ]\\nL24:     ALTER EXTENSION `name` SET SCHEMA `new_schema`\\nL25:     ALTER EXTENSION `name` ADD `member_object`\\nL26:     ALTER EXTENSION `name` DROP `member_object`\\nL27: \\nL28:     where `member_object` is:\\nL29: \\nL30:       ACCESS METHOD `object_name` |\\nL31:       AGGREGATE `aggregate_name` ( `aggregate_signature` ) |\\nL32:       CAST (`source_type` AS `target_type`) |\\nL33:       COLLATION `object_name` |\\nL34:       CONVERSION `object_name` |\\nL35:       DOMAIN `object_name` |\\nL36:       EVENT TRIGGER `object_name` |\\nL37:       FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER `object_name` |\\nL38:       FOREIGN TABLE `object_name` |\\nL39:       FUNCTION `function_name` [ ( [ [ `argmode` ] [ `argname` ] `argtype` [, ...] ] ) ] |\\nL40:       MATERIALIZED VIEW `object_name` |\\nL41:       OPERATOR `operator_name` (`left_type`, `right_type`) |\\nL42:       OPERATOR CLASS `object_name` USING `index_method` |\\nL43:       OPERATOR FAMILY `object_name` USING `index_method` |\\nL44:       [ PROCEDURAL ] LANGUAGE `object_name` |\\nL45:       PROCEDURE `procedure_name` [ ( [ [ `argmode` ] [ `argname` ] `argtype` [, ...] ] ) ] |\\nL46:       ROUTINE `routine_name` [ ( [ [ `argmode` ] [ `argname` ] `argtype` [, ...] ] ) ] |\\nL47:       SCHEMA `object_name` |\\nL48:       SEQUENCE `object_name` |\\nL49:       SERVER `object_name` |\\nL50:       TABLE `object_name` |\\nL51:       TEXT SEARCH CONFIGURATION `object_name` |\\nL52:       TEXT SEARCH DICTIONARY `object_name` |\\nL53:       TEXT SEARCH PARSER `object_name` |\\nL54:       TEXT SEARCH TEMPLATE `object_name` |\\nL55:       TRANSFORM FOR `type_name` LANGUAGE `lang_name` |\\nL56:       TYPE `object_name` |\\nL57:       VIEW `object_name`\\nL58: \\nL59:     and `aggregate_signature` is:\\nL60: \\nL61:     * |\\nL62:     [ `argmode` ] [ `argname` ] `argtype` [ , ... ] |\\nL63:     [ [ `argmode` ] [ `argname` ] `argtype` [ , ... ] ] ORDER BY [ `argmode` ] [ `argname` ] `argtype` [ , ... ]\\nL64: ## Description\\nL65: \\nL66: `ALTER EXTENSION` changes the definition of an installed extension. There are several subforms:\\nL67: \\nL68: `UPDATE`\\nL69: \\nL70: \\nL71: This form updates the extension to a newer version. The extension must supply a suitable update script (or series of scripts) that can modify the currently-installed version into the requested version.\\nL72: \\nL73: `SET SCHEMA`\\nL74: \\nL75: \\nL76: This form moves the extension's objects into another schema. The extension has to be relocatable for this command to succeed.\\nL77: \\nL78: `ADD `member_object``\\nL79: This form adds an existing object to the extension. This is mainly useful in extension update scripts. The object will subsequently be treated as a member of the extension; notably, it can only be dropped by dropping the extension.\\nL80: \\nL81: `DROP `member_object``\\nL82: \\nL83: \\nL84: This form removes a member object from the extension. This is mainly useful in extension update scripts. The object is not dropped, only disassociated from the extension.\\nL85: \\nL86: See cite24†Section 36.17 for more information about these operations.\\nL87: You must own the extension to use `ALTER EXTENSION`. The `ADD`/`DROP` forms require ownership of the added/dropped object as well.\\nL88: ## Parameters\\nL89: \\nL90: `name`\\nL91: \\nL92: \\nL93: The name of an installed extension.\\nL94: \\nL95: `new_version`\\nL96: \\nL97: \\nL98: The desired new version of the extension. This can be written as either an identifier or a string literal. If not specified, `ALTER EXTENSION UPDATE` attempts to update to whatever is shown as the default version in the extension's control file.\\nL99: \\nL100: `new_schema`\\nL101: \\nL102: \\nL103: The new schema for the extension.\\nL104: \\nL105: `object_name`\\nL106: `aggregate_name`\\nL107: `function_name`\\nL108: `operator_name`\\nL109: `procedure_name`\\nL110: `routine_name`\\nL111: The name of an object to be added to or removed from the extension. Names of tables, aggregates, domains, foreign tables, functions, operators, operator classes, operator families, procedures, routines, sequences, text search objects, types, and views can be schema-qualified.\\nL112: \\nL113: `source_type`\\nL114: \\nL115: \\nL116: The name of the source data type of the cast.\\nL117: \\nL118: `target_type`\\nL119: \\nL120: \\nL121: The name of the target data type of the cast.\\nL122: \\nL123: `argmode`\\nL124: The mode of a function, procedure, or aggregate argument: `IN`, `OUT`, `INOUT`, or `VARIADIC`. If omitted, the default is `IN`. Note that `ALTER EXTENSION` does not actually pay any attention to `OUT` arguments, since only the input arguments are needed to determine the function's identity. So it is sufficient to list the `IN`, `INOUT`, and `VARIADIC` arguments.\\nL125: \\nL126: `argname`\\nL127: The name of a function, procedure, or aggregate argument. Note that `ALTER EXTENSION` does not actually pay any attention to argument names, since only the argument data types are needed to determine the function's identity.\\nL128: \\nL129: `argtype`\\nL130: \\nL131: \\nL132: The data type of a function, procedure, or aggregate argument.\\nL133: \\nL134: `left_type`\\nL135: `right_type`\\nL136: \\nL137: \\nL138: The data type(s) of the operator's arguments (optionally schema-qualified). Write `NONE` for the missing argument of a prefix operator.\\nL139: \\nL140: `PROCEDURAL`\\nL141: This is a noise word.\\nL142: \\nL143: `type_name`\\nL144: \\nL145: \\nL146: The name of the data type of the transform.\\nL147: \\nL148: `lang_name`\\nL149: \\nL150: \\nL151: The name of the language of the transform.\\nL152: ## Examples\\nL153: \\nL154: To update the `hstore` extension to version 2.0:\\nL155: \\nL156:     ALTER EXTENSION hstore UPDATE TO '2.0';\\nL157: \\nL158: \\nL159: To change the schema of the `hstore` extension to `utils`:\\nL160: \\nL161:     ALTER EXTENSION hstore SET SCHEMA utils;\\nL162: \\nL163: \\nL164: To add an existing function to the `hstore` extension:\\nL165: \\nL166:     ALTER EXTENSION hstore ADD FUNCTION populate_record(anyelement, hstore);\\nL167: \\nL168: \\nL169: ## Compatibility\\nL170: \\nL171: `ALTER EXTENSION` is a PostgreSQL extension.\\nL172: ## See Also\\nL173: \\nL174: cite25†CREATE EXTENSION , cite26†DROP EXTENSION L175: \\nL176: * * *\\nL177: \\nL178: cite21†Prev | cite22†Up |  cite23†Next L179: ALTER EVENT TRIGGER   | cite2†Home |  ALTER FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER\\nL180: ## Submit correction\\nL181: \\nL182: If you see anything in the documentation that is not correct, does not match your experience with the particular feature or requires further clarification, please use cite27†this form to report a documentation issue.\\nL183: \\nL184: cite28†Policies | cite29†Code of Conduct | cite30†About PostgreSQL | cite31†Contact L185: \\nL186: Copyright © 1996-2026 The PostgreSQL Global Development Group\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ccb0-75a7-7862-941a-ed4430a5b3b7","create_time":1790154748.104353}}}
